CH = dict(num=8, title="The Gupta Era: An Age of Tireless Creativity", tag="History", theme="Guptas, administration, science, arts, regional kingdoms",
 intro="From the 3rd to the 6th century CE the Guptas gave north India a long spell of peace and prosperity. Mathematics, astronomy, medicine, metallurgy, literature and art reached new heights, so historians often call it the 'classical age'. Meanwhile the Vakatakas, Pallavas and Kamarupa flourished elsewhere.",
 big_questions=["Who were the Guptas and why is their period called the 'classical age'?", "What was happening in the rest of the Subcontinent?", "Who were the great figures of this period and why do they matter today?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "8.1 The Political Map (3rd-6th century CE)"),
 ("table", ["Kingdom", "Capital", "Area / note"], [
  ["Gupta Empire", "Pataliputra (Patna)", "Most powerful; 3rd to 6th century CE; north and west India plus parts of centre and east"],
  ["Vakatakas", "Nandivardhana (near Nagpur)", "Central India; **allies** of the Guptas"],
  ["Pallavas", "Kanchipuram", "South (Tamil Nadu, Karnataka, Telangana, Andhra); rose after the Satavahanas; ruled to the later 9th century"],
  ["Kamarupa (Varman dynasty)", "—", "Assam / Brahmaputra valley, north Bengal, Bangladesh"]]),
 ("h2", "8.2 The Iron Pillar of Delhi"),
 ("ul", ["At Mehrauli, Delhi; about **1,600 years old**, **6 tonnes**, **does not rust**. Erected in the reign of **Chandragupta II**, dedicated to **Vishnu**, with inscription praising the king 'Chandra'; probably first set up near the Udayagiri caves (MP).",
  "Reason for rust-resistance: a thin protective layer formed by the special iron and air's oxygen on the surface. A proof of India's advanced **metallurgy**."]),
 ("h2", "8.3 Rise and Rulers of the Guptas"),
 ("ul", ["The Kushana empire weakened by the 3rd century CE; the Guptas, probably regional rulers near present Uttar Pradesh, rose to power.",
  "**Chandragupta I** (the grandfather): early expansion, coins showing himself with Queen **Kumaradevi** and Lakshmi, strategic alliances.",
  "**Samudragupta** (warrior king): the **Prayaga Prashasti** (pillar inscription at Prayagraj by court poet **Harisena**) says he aimed to be **dharani-bandha**, 'unifier of the Earth'. He defeated many kings; some were reinstated as tributaries. Also patron of art and learning; coins show him playing the **veena**; ashvamedha coins mark the horse sacrifice. In the south he defeated a Pallava ruler and in the north-east the ruler of Kamarupa but let them keep their thrones if they paid tribute.",
  "**Chandragupta II 'Vikramaditya'** (Samudragupta's son): a Vishnu devotee (Garuda on inscriptions); golden age patron of poets, scholars, artists. Do not confuse with Chandragupta Maurya.",
  "Sources: the *Vishnu Purana* names core regions (Anuganga, Prayaga, Saketa/Ayodhya, Magadha); at its peak the empire was much larger."]),
 ("h2", "8.4 Faxian's Account"),
 ("ul", ["**Faxian** (Chinese pilgrim, early 5th century CE, AD 399-414) came to see Buddhist sites, learn from scholars and collect manuscripts. His *A Record of Buddhistic Kingdoms* describes numerous, happy people, light taxes (royal farmers paid a share of grain), salaried royal guards, rich orderly cities, **charitable houses and medical aid** set up by Vaishya heads of families.",
  "He also records the harsh treatment of the **chandalas** (outcastes living outside cities). Lesson: a traveller's account shows only one viewpoint; historians must **corroborate** with many sources."]),
 ("h2", "8.5 Governance, Titles and Alliances"),
 ("ul", ["**New titles**: *maharajadhiraja, samrat, chakravartin* (earlier kings used *rajan, maharaja*), showing claim to supreme authority.",
  "**Expansion**: war, diplomacy and **matrimonial alliances** (Kautilya's *mitra*). Example: **Prabhavati Gupta**, daughter of Chandragupta II, married a Vakataka prince; after he died young she was **regent** (temporary ruler) for her sons and kept the alliance firm. Her inscription calls her 'mother of two kings'; a Vishnu devotee, she built temples (e.g., Ramtek / Ramagiri).",
  "**Administration**: empire divided into provinces; **land grants** to local rulers, priests, chieftains, recorded on **copper plates**, ensuring tax collection and local control."]),
 ("h2", "8.6 Trade and Revenue"),
 ("ul", ["Main income: **land tax**; also fines, mines, irrigation, trade and crafts. Spent on administration, army, temples, infrastructure, scholars and artists.",
  "Trade with the Mediterranean, Southeast Asia and China (textiles, spices, ivory, gems) via the Indian Ocean network; **Socotra Island** (Arabian Sea) was a stop, with Indian traders' Brahmi inscriptions and stupa designs found there."]),
 ("h2", "8.7 The Classical Age"),
 ("ul", ["Gupta kings worshipped Vishnu yet supported Buddhists too: **Nalanda university** and viharas. Long **peace and stability** funded scholars and artists, so the era is called the **'classical age'**. Earlier knowledge was compiled into texts."]),
 ("table", ["Figure", "Field / place", "Contribution"], [
  ["Aryabhata (c. 500 CE)", "Mathematics, astronomy; Kusumapura (near Patna)", "*Aryabhatiya*; formulas for motion of Sun, Moon, planets; Earth **spins on its axis** (day and night); year = 365 days 6 h 12 min 30 s (modern: 365 d 5 h 48 min 45 s); fair size of Earth; correct explanation of **eclipses**; calculation methods"],
  ["Varahamihira", "Astronomy, mathematics, astrology; Ujjayini", "*Brihat Samhita*: encyclopedia of astronomy, weather forecasting, architecture, town planning, agriculture"],
  ["Kalidasa", "Sanskrit poet", "*Meghadutam* ('Cloud Messenger'): a banished yaksha sends a message to his beloved through a cloud, describing landscapes and weather of north India; also *Raghuvamsham*"],
  ["Charaka and Sushruta (texts compiled)", "Ayurveda", "*Charaka Samhita* and *Sushruta Samhita* given final form: diseases, diagnosis, diet, medicines, surgery; holistic mind-body-nature view"]]),
 ("h2", "8.8 Art and Architecture"),
 ("ul", ["**Sarnath**: exquisite Buddha sculptures. **Ajanta caves** (Maharashtra; carved with Gupta and Vakataka support): rock-cut caves, central stupa with Buddha, wall paintings such as Bodhisattva **Padmapani**, Jataka tales. **Udayagiri** (MP): rock-cut caves with carvings (Vishnu etc.). **Deogarh Dashavatara temple** (UP): Vishnu on Sheshnag. Terracotta Ganga (on makara) and Yamuna (on tortoise) from Ahichchhatra. Gupta art set high standards of beauty."]),
 ("h2", "8.9 Decline and Neighbours"),
 ("ul", ["By the 6th century CE the **Huna** tribes from Central Asia attacked repeatedly, and strong regional rulers rose, ending Gupta power.",
  "**Pallavas**: possibly tributaries of the Satavahanas; mostly Shiva devotees; great temple and rock-cut builders; Kanchipuram, 'city of a thousand temples', a major centre of learning with **ghatikas** (centres of learning begun under the Satavahanas).",
  "**Kamarupa** (Varmans): ancient name **Pragjyotisha**; the Mahabharata mentions king **Bhagadatta**; temples and monasteries were centres of learning. Both are in the Prayaga Prashasti.",
  "Pattern: the Guptas defeated rulers but let them rule as tributaries."]),
 ("h2", "8.10 Quick Revision"),
 ("qa", [("Why is the Gupta age called the classical age?", "Long peace allowed achievements in literature, science, art and medicine, and earlier knowledge was compiled."),
  ("Who was Vikramaditya?", "Chandragupta II."),
  ("What does the Prayaga Prashasti tell us?", "Harisena's inscription praises Samudragupta's conquests and patronage; he aimed to 'unify the Earth'."),
  ("What is special about the Iron Pillar?", "A 6-tonne, 1,600-year-old pillar that has not rusted due to a protective surface layer; shows ancient metallurgy."),
  ("Who was Prabhavati Gupta?", "Daughter of Chandragupta II, Vakataka queen and regent who kept the Gupta-Vakataka alliance strong."),
  ("How did the Guptas administer their empire?", "Provinces and land grants to local leaders, recorded on copper plates, for tax collection and local control."),
  ("What were the main sources of Gupta revenue?", "Land tax, fines, taxes on mines, irrigation, trade and crafts."),
  ("List Aryabhata's achievements.", "Aryabhatiya; spinning Earth; correct eclipse explanation; accurate year length; calculation methods."),
  ("What is Brihat Samhita?", "Varahamihira's encyclopedic work on astronomy, weather, architecture, town planning and farming."),
  ("What does Meghadutam describe?", "A yaksha's message to his beloved through a cloud, with landscapes and weather of north India."),
  ("Who was Faxian and what did he report?", "A Chinese Buddhist pilgrim who described a prosperous, orderly society but also harsh treatment of chandalas."),
  ("Why did the Gupta Empire decline?", "Repeated Huna invasions and the rise of regional powers."),
  ("Who were the Pallavas?", "A southern dynasty (capital Kanchipuram) of temple builders, mostly Shiva devotees.")]),
 ],
 takeaways=["Guptas: 3rd-6th century CE, capital **Pataliputra**; key rulers **Chandragupta I, Samudragupta, Chandragupta II (Vikramaditya)**.",
  "Governance by provinces, **land grants**, matrimonial alliances; main revenue **land tax**.",
  "**Aryabhata, Varahamihira, Kalidasa, Charaka-Sushruta** and the **Iron Pillar** show scientific and literary brilliance.",
  "Art: **Ajanta, Sarnath, Udayagiri, Deogarh**.",
  "Decline due to **Hunas** and regional powers.",
  "Contemporaries: **Vakatakas, Pallavas, Kamarupa**."])
